All plants lived in build/k4-scratch, which is a `git archive` copy, so the
repository working tree was never edited for this review.


5. Findings
-----------
F1 (blocking, gate).  Dropping the `s.gte(&l)` rejection still exits 33.  The
selftest has no non-canonical-S case although its own doc comment claims one,
and the committed fixture already ships a ready "S + L (malleable encoding)"
negative for vector 0.  This is the exact failure RFC 8032 5.1.7 warns about,
and it is the failure mode the owner asked to see caught.

F2 (blocking, gate).  Accepting an all-zero public key still exits 33.  The
code's behaviour is correct today (the host harness rejects it, and the
`s.gte(&l)`/decode path would reject a zero signature), but the gate cannot
tell a correct implementation from one that special-cases the key to true.

F3 (claim vs code).  trust.rs:1-5 says "The kernel refuses to execute any ELF
whose package manifest signature or SHA-256 digest does not verify against the
embedded root authority."  No code path calls the verifier:

  $ grep -rn 'verify_manifest_signature\|verify_package_elf\|ed25519_verify\|trust::sha256\|trust::sha512' kernel/src --include=*.rs | grep -v '^kernel/src/trust'
  (no callers outside trust.rs/trustselftest.rs)

  verify_package_elf and verify_manifest_signature are exercised only by the
  selftest.  Nothing gates an ELF load or an install on them yet, so the
  invariant is not enforced in the boot path.  The module doc should not claim
  enforcement until it exists (or K4 should wire it in).

F4 (coverage).  The fixture ships 12 negatives per vector, including
"R with y >= p (non-canonical)" and "S + L"; the in-kernel gate uses 3.  The
host harness shows all 12 are rejected by the implementation, so the code is
stronger than its gate.

F5 (thin SHA-2 coverage in-kernel).  Only sha256(""), sha256(fox) and
sha512("") are pinned.  The multi-block 448-bit/896-bit messages and the
112/128-byte padding boundary are only covered on the host (they pass), so a
regression that only affects block-boundary padding would not fail the gate.


6. What I could not do
----------------------
- Timing side channels.  No constant-time analysis was done.  scalar_mul is a
  plain double-and-add with a data-dependent `if (word & 1) != 0` branch, and
  inv_mod_p is a full 256-bit square-and-multiply, so secret-dependent timing
  is plausible.  This only matters if signature verification ever handles
  attacker-chosen secrets; signing is not in the kernel.
- Small-order points beyond the all-zero key.  I did not construct a full
  forgery against a small-order public key, so I can neither confirm nor deny
  that the cofactorless equation (no multiplication by 8) is exploitable here,
  beyond the all-zero-key plant above.
- Fuzzing.  Malformed encodings, huge manifests (the 1024-byte buffer limit),
  and adversarial stack depth were not fuzzed; no crash was observed in the
  cases run.
- Real firmware and hardware.  Only QEMU 11.1.0 with SeaBIOS and OVMF.
- The process table / capability gate beyond the 7 checks the serial shows.
  This review is about trust.rs and trustselftest.rs only.
- Whether the selftest gate can be reached from a non-`novatest` boot.  It is
  gated on the command line; a normal boot does not run it.

WHAT I AM UNSURE OF
-------------------
- Whether the 1024-byte signing buffer is intended as a hard size cap for
  manifests; no test pins the boundary.
- Whether F3 (no caller) is in scope for K4 or belongs to the K7 install path.
  I am reporting it as a doc/claim mismatch, not as a broken loader.


VERDICT: FAIL (constants and algorithms verified against OpenSSL/hashlib/RFC
8032; 2 of the 4 required plants still exit 33, so the gate does not detect
non-canonical-S malleability or an all-zero public key, and the verifier has no
caller in the boot path although its module doc claims enforcement)


