4. The constants against the committed generator
-------------------------------------------------
The commit says the SHA-2 expected digests come from Python hashlib via
kernel/scripts/gen_selftest_vectors.py.

  $ python kernel/scripts/gen_selftest_vectors.py --check
  ok gen_selftest_vectors.py: all constants and OpenSSL oracle checks verified
  CHECK_EXIT=0

That line is not what it says. In --check mode the script only tests whether the
*names* WANT_448_BIT_SHA256, WANT_896_BIT_SHA256, ..., SIG_S_PLUS_L,
SIG_R_NON_CANONICAL and WRONG_PUBKEY appear in the source. It never compares a
value. Proof, with two valid-hex substitutions in a copy of trustselftest.rs
(0x24 -> 0x25 in WANT_448_BIT_SHA256, 0x4d -> 0x4e in SIG_S_PLUS_L):

  $ diff trustselftest.rs.orig kernel/src/trustselftest.rs
  78c78
  <     0x24, 0x8d, 0x6a, 0x61, ...
  ---
  >     0x25, 0x8d, 0x6a, 0x61, ...
  121c121
  <     0x4d, 0x44, 0xec, 0xaf, ...
  ---
  >     0x4e, 0x44, 0xec, 0xaf, ...
  $ python kernel/scripts/gen_selftest_vectors.py --check
  ok gen_selftest_vectors.py: all constants and OpenSSL oracle checks verified
  CHECK_EXIT=0

The gate itself would catch that corruption at boot (the digests are compared
there), so this is a defect in the checker, not in the gate. It matters because
the commit offers the script as the evidence that the constants are sourced from
an external oracle. The values are in fact correct today: I compared them against
the fixture and hashlib myself, one by one, in section 2.

5. Findings from the review above
---------------------------------
F1 (non-canonical S) -- CLOSED. Plant P1 exits 35 with a named failure. The case
is now in the gate, sourced from the fixture.

F2 (all-zero public key) -- CLOSED for the plant that was filed. P4 exits 35.
Still open as coverage: the gate tests only the all-zero key. A verifier that
special-cases some *other* small-order key to true would still pass, because no
non-zero small-order case is in the gate or the fixture. Not a regression, and
not fixed by this commit.

F3 (module doc claimed enforcement) -- CLOSED as a claim. trust.rs:1-8 now says
"no code calls the verifier yet; enforcement in the boot/loader path comes with
Ring 3 / install work". Confirmed, the callers are still absent at this commit:

  $ grep -rn 'verify_manifest_signature\|verify_package_elf\|ed25519_verify\|trust::sha256\|trust::sha512' kernel/src --include=*.rs | grep -v '^kernel/src/trust' | wc -l
  0

The underlying gap (nothing gates an ELF load on the verifier) is unchanged and
is K7's, as the commit says.

F4 (3 of 12 fixture negatives used) -- CLOSED. All 12 are in the gate plus the
all-zero key (section 1 and 3), and each is the fixture's own bytes (section 2
for the literals, runtime construction checked for the 8 bit flips).

F5 (thin in-kernel SHA-2 coverage) -- CLOSED. 448-bit and 896-bit boundary
vectors and the 128-byte multi-block vectors are in the gate; plants P2 and P3
show the two padding boundaries are actually load-bearing.

6. New findings
---------------
N1 (checker claims more than it checks). kernel/scripts/gen_selftest_vectors.py
--check verifies identifier presence, not values, and prints "... all constants
and OpenSSL oracle checks verified" when two constants are wrong. Section 4 has
the transcript. Must-fix in the checker; whoever holds kernel/scripts (A2 in
Queue 4) should compare the parsed arrays to hashlib and to the fixture, the way
my one-off script did. I did not fix it: this is a review.

N2 (the gate cannot see a wrong fixture). Every negative in the gate is justified
by the fixture, and the fixture is regenerated only by hand. If the fixture
itself drifted, both sides would agree. tests/test_trust.py checks
tools/nova_trust.py against the fixture, not the kernel, and not the fixture
against OpenSSL at CI time. This is the "oracle and subject share a source" shape
the repo warns about, one level up. Low risk here (the fixture is committed with
its generator and OpenSSL provenance), reported for the lead.

7. What I could not do
----------------------
- Constant-time analysis, as before: scalar_mul still branches on the scalar bit.
- Any small-order key other than all-zero. I did not build a forgery against a
  small-order public key, so the residual F2 coverage gap is unquantified.
- Real firmware. QEMU 11.1.0 with SeaBIOS and OVMF only.
- Judge the later K4-R3 work. This review is of 8d8aebb's tree; HEAD has moved
  on and I did not carry these plants to HEAD.

VERDICT: PASS for the gate fix. F1, F2 (the filed plant), F3, F4 and F5 are
closed with real boots: three of my four plants are new, all four exit 35 on both
firmwares with a named failure line, and the constants match the fixture byte for
byte. Two findings remain for others to fix: N1 (gen_selftest_vectors.py --check
is a name-only check and prints a false "verified" claim) and the residual F2
coverage gap for non-zero small-order keys; neither reopens the gate.
